% bbdd-session(8) | Linux

NAME
====

`bbdd session` - manage BFD sessions

SYNOPSIS
========

```
bbdd session add [ SET-PARAMS ]
bbdd session [ QUERY-PARAMS ] [bulk] set [ SET-PARAMS ]
bbdd session [ QUERY-PARAMS ] [bulk] del
bbdd session [ QUERY-PARAMS ] show
bbdd session [ QUERY-PARAMS ] [diag] stats
```

DESCRIPTION
===========

The `session` command manages BFD sessions. Each invocation has up to two
independent sets of parameters:

`QUERY-PARAMS`
: Select which sessions the command operates on.

`SET-PARAMS`
: Describe the configuration to apply.

Whether an operation is permitted to affect more than one session at a
time is controlled by the `bulk` modifier. The individual subcommands
interpret these inputs as follows:

`add`
: No query parameters are accepted. The `SET-PARAMS` describe the
  configuration of the newly created session. `bulk` is not applicable;
  at most one session is created per invocation.

`set`
: The `QUERY-PARAMS` select the sessions to modify. The `SET-PARAMS`
  describe the changes to apply. Without `bulk`, matching more than one
  session is an error.

`del`
: The `QUERY-PARAMS` select the sessions to delete. No `SET-PARAMS` are
  accepted. Without `bulk`, matching more than one session is an error.

`show`
: The `QUERY-PARAMS` select the sessions to display. No `SET-PARAMS`
  are accepted. Always operates in bulk.

`stats`
: The `QUERY-PARAMS` select the sessions whose counters to display. The
  `diag` modifier selects diagnostic counters instead of operational
  ones. Always operates in bulk.

PARAMETERS
==========

All parameters are usable as both `QUERY-PARAMS` and `SET-PARAMS` unless
noted otherwise.

`discr U32`

: Session discriminator. A 32-bit unsigned integer uniquely identifying the
  session within `bbdd`. When not given for a new session, is chosen
  randomly and uniquely.

`src ADDR`, `no src`

: Source address of BFD control packets. When not configured, the source
  address is determined by the kernel routing lookup for each transmitted
  packet. `no src` clears the setting on an existing session.

`dst ADDR`

: Destination address of BFD control packets.

`min-tx TIME`

: Desired minimum transmit interval. The actual transmit interval is
  negotiated with the peer and may be longer than this value.

`min-rx TIME`

: Required minimum receive interval. The peer is expected to transmit at
  least this frequently; intervals below this are treated as a detection
  failure.

`detect-mult U8`

: Detection time multiplier. The detection time is this value multiplied by
  the negotiated receive interval. When no packet arrives within the
  detection time, the session is declared down.

`ttl U8`

: Minimum acceptable IP TTL for received packets. The default is 255.
  Single-hop sessions should use the default; multi-hop sessions typically
  use a lower value reflecting the expected hop count.

`netif IFNAME`, `netif-index U32`, `no netif`

: Network interface by name or index, respectively. Either or both may be
  given; the daemon resolves missing parts automatically. `no netif` clears
  the interface association on an existing session.

`vrf IFNAME`, `vrf-index U32`, `vrf-table U32`, `no vrf`

: VRF for packet forwarding, specified by interface name, interface index,
  or routing table ID, respectively. Any combination of the three forms is
  accepted.

  When the VRF name or index is given, the daemon can derive the routing
  table ID automatically. The reverse is not possible: the mapping from
  table IDs to VRF interfaces is not unique, so the daemon does not attempt
  to fill in the name or index when only `vrf-table` is given.

  `no vrf` clears both the VRF interface setting and the VRF table. `no vrf
  vrf-table U32` clears the VRF interface but sets a specific table for
  forwarding.

`hold-time TIME`

: Time to wait before activating a newly created session. During the hold
  period, the session exists in the daemon's data model but does not yet
  transmit or receive packets. Only meaningful as a `SET-PARAM` on `add`.

FLAGS
=====

Session flags may be set or cleared using `[no] FLAG` syntax. Without
`no`, the flag is set; with `no`, it is cleared.

`[no] multihop`

: Multi-hop session. Multi-hop sessions use UDP port 4784 instead of
  3784 and are not constrained to a single IP hop.

`[no] cpi`

: Control-Plane Independent. When set, the C bit is included in
  transmitted BFD packets, indicating that the forwarding plane
  continues to operate independently of the control plane.

`[no] passive`

: Passive mode. A passive session does not initiate the BFD handshake;
  it waits for the peer to send the first packet before responding.

`[no] shutdown`

: Administratively down.

PARAMETER TYPES
===============

`U8`, `U32`

: An 8-bit or 32-bit non-negative integral value.

`IFNAME`

: An interface name given as a string, e.g. `eth0`.

`ADDR`

: An IPv4 or IPv6 address notation. E.g. `192.0.2.1` or `2001:db8:1::1`.

`TIME`

: A time duration expressed either as a raw `U32` number of microseconds, or
  human readable notation such as `100us`, `10ms` or `1s`.

OUTPUT FORMAT
=============

## show

Each session is printed on one line. The configuration fields appear
first, followed by state sections separated by `|`.

The `local` section shows the daemon's own BFD state and diagnostic
code. The `remote` section shows the last-received values from the peer:
discriminator, detection multiplier, negotiated intervals, C bit, and
the peer's state and diagnostic code.

BFD states are: `admindown`, `down`, `init`, `up`.

```
# bbdd session show
discr 1 dst 192.0.2.2 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms detect-mult 3 netif veth1 vrf vrf1 | local state up diag nothing | remote discr 2 detect-mult 3 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms state up diag nothing
discr 2 dst 192.0.2.1 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms detect-mult 3 netif veth2 vrf vrf2 | local state up diag nothing | remote discr 1 detect-mult 3 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms state up diag nothing
```

With `-v` (`--verbose`), additional BPF-level state is shown in a `bpf`
section: the BPF session state (`bstate`), the effective negotiated
timing parameters (`eff-timing`), the timing parameters used during a
poll sequence (`poll-timing`), and whether timing is being queued for a
deferred update (`qd-timing`).

With `-v`, fields that have no value set (e.g. `no src`, `no netif`)
are also shown explicitly.

```
# bbdd -v session show
discr 1 no src dst 192.0.2.2 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms detect-mult 3 netif veth1 netif-index 171 vrf vrf1 vrf-index 2 vrf-table 1000 no multihop no cpi no passive no shutdown | local state up diag nothing | remote discr 2 detect-mult 3 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms no cpi state up diag nothing | bpf state stable eff-timing detect-mult 3 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms poll-timing detect-mult 0 min-tx 0s min-rx 0s 
discr 2 no src dst 192.0.2.1 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms detect-mult 3 netif veth2 netif-index 170 vrf vrf2 vrf-index 3 vrf-table 2000 no multihop no cpi no passive no shutdown | local state up diag nothing | remote discr 1 detect-mult 3 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms no cpi state up diag nothing | bpf state stable eff-timing detect-mult 3 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms poll-timing detect-mult 0 min-tx 0s min-rx 0s 
```

## stats

Each session's counters are printed with the discriminator on a heading
line, followed by the counter values. Operational counters include
received and transmitted packet and byte counts. Diagnostic counters
(`diag stats`) report internal exception event counts.

```
# bbdd session discr 1 stats
discr 1:
	rx_bytes: 8698140
	rx_packets: 131790
	tx_bytes: 8700450
	tx_packets: 131825
```

EXAMPLES
========

Create two sessions in different VRFs:

```
bbdd session add dst 192.0.2.1 vrf V1 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms detect-mult 3
bbdd session add dst 192.0.2.2 vrf V2 min-tx 200ms min-rx 200ms detect-mult 3
```

Raise the transmit and receive intervals on all sessions at once:

```
bbdd session bulk set min-tx 300ms min-rx 300ms
```

Delete all sessions in VRF V1:

```
bbdd session vrf V1 bulk del
```

Show operational and diagnostic counters for a specific session:

```
bbdd session dst 192.0.2.1 stats
bbdd session dst 192.0.2.1 diag stats
```

SEE ALSO
========

bbdd(8), bbdd-bfdd(8)
